WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.430
This is the executive briefing
for AI Identity Governance,

3
00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:07.874
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:11.710
It's for the people who make
the calls: the CIO, the CISO,

5
00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:17.375
the Chief AI Officer, authorizing officials,
the privacy officer and sponsors.

6
00:00:17.375 --> 00:00:18.800
The argument is short.

7
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.875
An AI system that can act, not
just answer, is an account.

8
00:00:22.875 --> 00:00:28.013
Most AI arrived outside the identity
program, with no owner and no end date.

9
00:00:28.013 --> 00:00:30.449
And there's no separate AI authorization,

10
00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:35.537
so those accounts belong in the boundary
your authorizing official already reviews.

11
00:00:35.537 --> 00:00:38.775
I'll take four questions:
why, what, who and how.

12
00:00:38.775 --> 00:00:43.150
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

13
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.880
Let's start with what
makes an agent an account.

14
00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:52.478
A chat assistant that
drafts a memo is a tool;

15
00:00:52.478 --> 00:00:55.405
nothing reaches a record
until a person acts.

16
00:00:55.405 --> 00:00:58.522
An agent reads the mailbox,
sends the replies,

17
00:00:58.522 --> 00:01:01.217
closes the ticket and updates the case.

18
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:06.105
Each step is an action, taken by something
that signed in and holds permissions.

19
00:01:06.105 --> 00:01:07.305
That's an identity.

20
00:01:07.305 --> 00:01:10.094
It can act for a signed-in
user, or as itself,

21
00:01:10.094 --> 00:01:13.130
and either way the
identity provider sees it.

22
00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.266
Or it can use a stored A P I key,

23
00:01:15.266 --> 00:01:18.980
and then no policy and no
sign-in log sees it at all.

24
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:24.564
Today only 117 of 3,611 reported
federal use cases are agentic,

25
00:01:24.564 --> 00:01:28.068
but that's the share that grows fastest.

26
00:01:28.068 --> 00:01:31.279
So count AI identities, not AI projects.

27
00:01:31.279 --> 00:01:33.340
Next, how they got here.

28
00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.849
People join through a pipeline:
an HR record, an account,

29
00:01:40.849 --> 00:01:44.190
access from their position,
and a leaver process.

30
00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.490
AI mostly didn't.

31
00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.872
A vendor pilot, an A P I
key in a configuration file,

32
00:01:48.872 --> 00:01:53.352
an assistant that shipped inside
software you already license.

33
00:01:53.352 --> 00:01:56.523
So it typically has no
owner, no placement,

34
00:01:56.523 --> 00:02:00.627
a stored secret, access nobody
reviews and no end date.

35
00:02:00.627 --> 00:02:02.765
And the risk is identity-shaped.

36
00:02:02.765 --> 00:02:06.741
A planted prompt against an agent
doesn't produce a bad answer;

37
00:02:06.741 --> 00:02:10.015
it produces an action,
under a legitimate identity.

38
00:02:10.015 --> 00:02:13.477
The blast radius is whatever
that identity can reach.

39
00:02:13.477 --> 00:02:17.199
So the first decision: an
agent with no governed identity

40
00:02:17.199 --> 00:02:20.840
is a high-priority finding,
and a person decides its fate.

41
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.260
The good news: the controls already exist.

42
00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.985
No new control family is needed.

43
00:02:29.985 --> 00:02:34.273
The security control catalog already
covers system and service accounts,

44
00:02:34.273 --> 00:02:38.561
identifiers and credentials for
services, least privilege for processes,

45
00:02:38.561 --> 00:02:41.397
and services that authenticate
before they talk.

46
00:02:41.397 --> 00:02:46.173
NIST's AI framework adds inventory,
decommissioning and clear roles.

47
00:02:46.173 --> 00:02:48.147
So the gap isn't the catalog.

48
00:02:48.147 --> 00:02:51.781
It's coverage: the AI system's
account is often simply missing

49
00:02:51.781 --> 00:02:55.560
from the account inventory those
controls are tested against.

50
00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.939
The book's model gives every AI identity
seven things, from an identity record and a

51
00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:06.398
named owner to a lifecycle that ends and
evidence the authorizing official can use.

52
00:03:06.398 --> 00:03:10.861
That model is program design, built
on controls you already implement.

53
00:03:10.861 --> 00:03:13.720
Now, what the federal rules actually say.

54
00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.833
Federal AI policy was rewritten in 2025.

55
00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:26.143
One OMB memo, M twenty-five twenty-one,
governs use: a Chief AI Officer,

56
00:03:26.143 --> 00:03:31.744
an annual use-case inventory, and
minimum practices for high-impact AI.

57
00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:37.047
Its companion, M twenty-five twenty-two,
governs buying AI, and says plainly that

58
00:03:37.047 --> 00:03:42.757
AI run as an information system needs an
authorization to operate before it deploys.

59
00:03:42.757 --> 00:03:45.784
The 2026 executive orders harden federal

60
00:03:45.784 --> 00:03:49.244
systems and change
vocabulary, not obligations.

61
00:03:49.244 --> 00:03:51.883
What the rules don't do
matters just as much.

62
00:03:51.883 --> 00:03:54.176
There's no AI-specific authorization,

63
00:03:54.176 --> 00:03:57.208
and the memo sets no identity
requirements at all.

64
00:03:57.208 --> 00:03:59.444
So don't build a second approval track.

65
00:03:59.444 --> 00:04:02.450
Put the AI system's accounts
in the boundary your

66
00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:06.057
authorizing official already
reviews, before it deploys.

67
00:04:06.057 --> 00:04:09.080
High-impact AI adds one more signature.

68
00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:15.668
High-impact AI is the
kind whose output drives

69
00:04:15.668 --> 00:04:18.655
decisions with real effects
on rights or safety.

70
00:04:18.655 --> 00:04:24.557
For it, the memo requires seven minimum
practices: testing, an impact assessment,

71
00:04:24.557 --> 00:04:29.743
monitoring, training, oversight with
a fail-safe, appeals, and feedback.

72
00:04:29.743 --> 00:04:32.731
The impact assessment ends
with a signature from the

73
00:04:32.731 --> 00:04:35.992
official accepting the risk,
after an independent review.

74
00:04:35.992 --> 00:04:37.167
Here's the catch.

75
00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:41.129
That signature isn't an
authorization, and the memo says so.

76
00:04:41.129 --> 00:04:45.323
A high-impact use case needs both:
the signed risk acceptance and the

77
00:04:45.323 --> 00:04:49.517
system's authorization to operate,
often from two different people.

78
00:04:49.517 --> 00:04:52.962
So the decision is to put both
decision-makers on the same

79
00:04:52.962 --> 00:04:56.554
record of what the system is, who
owns it and what it can reach.

80
00:04:56.554 --> 00:04:58.460
Where does that record start?

81
00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:07.260
It starts with the use-case inventory,
the one AI list every agency keeps.

82
00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.568
Read as identity data, it
gives you a stable ID,

83
00:05:10.568 --> 00:05:15.485
an office and a lifecycle stage,
where retired means a leaver event.

84
00:05:15.485 --> 00:05:20.560
It leaves blank everything that decides
what an AI system can touch: the account

85
00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.173
it signs in as, its credential, its
permissions and its accountable owner.

86
00:05:25.173 --> 00:05:27.784
And its high-impact fields report status.

87
00:05:27.784 --> 00:05:33.185
They say yes or in progress; they don't
carry a log, a document or a name.

88
00:05:33.185 --> 00:05:35.147
So treat it as the seed list.

89
00:05:35.147 --> 00:05:39.075
The decision belongs to the
Chief AI Officer: no use case is

90
00:05:39.075 --> 00:05:43.822
recorded as piloted or deployed
without a link to its identity record.

91
00:05:43.822 --> 00:05:46.120
Next, what kind of identity.

92
00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:52.633
An AI system's identity isn't the model.

93
00:05:52.633 --> 00:05:56.369
It's the software around it,
sorted by whose token it presents.

94
00:05:56.369 --> 00:05:59.932
A signed-in user's, so it can
do everything that user can.

95
00:05:59.932 --> 00:06:02.886
Its own, as a workload or agent identity,

96
00:06:02.886 --> 00:06:06.670
where today the only policy
control is to block it.

97
00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:08.958
Or no token at all, just a key.

98
00:06:08.958 --> 00:06:14.889
Microsoft's agent identity platform
became generally available in April 2026,

99
00:06:14.889 --> 00:06:19.742
but the agent policies are still
preview, G C C has a partial subset,

100
00:06:19.742 --> 00:06:23.607
and GCC High and DoD have
no published availability.

101
00:06:23.607 --> 00:06:27.408
And licensing governance for people
doesn't license it for agents.

102
00:06:27.408 --> 00:06:30.951
So the CIO budgets the
licenses, and every capability

103
00:06:30.951 --> 00:06:35.020
is confirmed in your own cloud
before anyone plans on it.

104
00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.866
An agent has no hire date
and no separation date,

105
00:06:41.866 --> 00:06:45.120
so its lifecycle has to hang
from a person who does.

106
00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.745
Left to the defaults, the sponsor is
whoever built it, often a developer.

107
00:06:49.745 --> 00:06:54.613
The book's rule is two named humans and
a record: a federal employee as sponsor,

108
00:06:54.613 --> 00:06:58.709
a technical owner who holds the
credentials but never sponsors,

109
00:06:58.709 --> 00:07:02.032
and an inventory record that
names the same sponsor.

110
00:07:02.032 --> 00:07:05.799
When the sponsor leaves,
sponsorship moves to their manager,

111
00:07:05.799 --> 00:07:10.470
who has ten business days to confirm,
or the agent is orphaned and suspended.

112
00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:13.695
And access expires unless
the sponsor renews it.

113
00:07:13.695 --> 00:07:17.771
The decision is the system owner's:
name that sponsor at creation,

114
00:07:17.771 --> 00:07:21.140
and move it to the using
program at publication.

115
00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:27.601
An agent can't be asked
for a second factor,

116
00:07:27.601 --> 00:07:30.790
so everything it may do is
decided before it asks.

117
00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:35.990
Credentials first: a managed identity or
federation, so there's no stored secret;

118
00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:41.190
a certificate only where neither works;
and a client secret never in production.

119
00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:42.440
Then permissions.

120
00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:45.402
Microsoft blocks the very
top roles for agents,

121
00:07:45.402 --> 00:07:49.352
but still lets one hold Exchange
or SharePoint administrator.

122
00:07:49.352 --> 00:07:51.677
That's a floor, not least privilege.

123
00:07:51.677 --> 00:07:56.188
So the book's rule is no standing
privileged role for any AI identity,

124
00:07:56.188 --> 00:07:59.733
and a policy that blocks
every agent not yet approved,

125
00:07:59.733 --> 00:08:02.552
kept in report-only mode
while it's preview.

126
00:08:02.552 --> 00:08:04.578
The CISO sets those requirements,

127
00:08:04.578 --> 00:08:09.200
and identity engineering builds them,
before the first production agent.

128
00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:17.025
An AI identity nobody watches
drifts the day after it's issued,

129
00:08:17.025 --> 00:08:20.062
and AI nobody declared
is drift from the start.

130
00:08:20.062 --> 00:08:23.589
So the program compares three
things: what was declared,

131
00:08:23.589 --> 00:08:27.575
what known identities are seen
doing, and what discovery finds.

132
00:08:27.575 --> 00:08:31.756
Shadow AI shows up in four
places: AI services staff use,

133
00:08:31.756 --> 00:08:38.075
apps holding consented access, agents on
endpoints, and AI built in your own clouds.

134
00:08:38.075 --> 00:08:42.025
No single tool sees all four,
and none sees a static key.

135
00:08:42.025 --> 00:08:45.168
Each gap becomes a finding
with an owner and a clock,

136
00:08:45.168 --> 00:08:47.488
and the serious ones halt for a person.

137
00:08:47.488 --> 00:08:50.324
Security operations contains shadow AI.

138
00:08:50.324 --> 00:08:56.062
Then the system owner decides its future,
or the CIO, when it belongs to no system.

139
00:08:56.062 --> 00:08:57.550
So who owns it?

140
00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:05.824
Ask who owns an agent's identity and the
AI office points at the identity team,

141
00:09:05.824 --> 00:09:07.619
the identity team points back,

142
00:09:07.619 --> 00:09:11.712
and the program office says it
already signed the impact assessment.

143
00:09:11.712 --> 00:09:13.451
The answer is two tracks.

144
00:09:13.451 --> 00:09:16.419
The Chief AI Officer
owns the declared side:

145
00:09:16.419 --> 00:09:20.213
the inventory, high-impact
determinations and waivers.

146
00:09:20.213 --> 00:09:24.487
The authorizing official alone
accepts the system's residual risk.

147
00:09:24.487 --> 00:09:26.566
Neither stands in for the other,

148
00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:30.963
and AI adds no new authorizing
official and no new account manager.

149
00:09:30.963 --> 00:09:34.706
A few separations keep it honest;
for example, the official who

150
00:09:34.706 --> 00:09:39.037
waived a practice shouldn't be that
system's sole authorizing official.

151
00:09:39.037 --> 00:09:41.475
And one rule underpins everything:

152
00:09:41.475 --> 00:09:45.676
the identity record exists
before any credential is issued.

153
00:09:45.676 --> 00:09:48.610
The identity governance
officer decides that.

154
00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:56.097
Here's the matrix in one view,
with one decider per row.

155
00:09:56.097 --> 00:10:01.236
The Chief AI Officer owns the inventory,
the high-impact calls and waivers.

156
00:10:01.236 --> 00:10:04.672
The use-case risk acceptor
signs the impact assessment;

157
00:10:04.672 --> 00:10:07.885
the authorizing official
signs the authorization.

158
00:10:07.885 --> 00:10:10.595
The CISO sets the control requirements,

159
00:10:10.595 --> 00:10:14.582
the CIO owns licenses and shadow
AI that belongs to no system,

160
00:10:14.582 --> 00:10:19.285
and the system owner names the sponsor
and decides what the AI may reach.

161
00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:21.831
Contractors can build and run agents;

162
00:10:21.831 --> 00:10:25.772
government officials sponsor,
authorize and accept risk.

163
00:10:25.772 --> 00:10:29.422
And because agents change
quickly, one decision recurs.

164
00:10:29.422 --> 00:10:33.817
Every new tool, permission, user
account or model is raised by the system

165
00:10:33.817 --> 00:10:38.660
owner as a possible significant change,
and the authorizing official decides.

166
00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:40.750
Now, finding the gaps.

167
00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:49.459
The inventory scan is cheap, because it
reads answers agencies have already given.

168
00:10:49.459 --> 00:10:54.559
It's useful only if every answer it questions
ends with an owner who goes and looks.

169
00:10:54.559 --> 00:10:59.777
Run over the published 2025 file, the
simple checks hit 72 percent of the live

170
00:10:59.777 --> 00:11:05.332
entries: more than half give no authorization
answer, and a third name no contact.

171
00:11:05.332 --> 00:11:09.227
Those are answers to reconcile,
not verdicts on agencies.

172
00:11:09.227 --> 00:11:13.428
The checks that matter most, like
an agent with no identity record,

173
00:11:13.428 --> 00:11:16.616
need the directory, so only
the agency can run them.

174
00:11:16.616 --> 00:11:21.433
The decision: run the checks on your own
submission every vintage, agentic and

175
00:11:21.433 --> 00:11:23.389
authorization findings first,

176
00:11:23.389 --> 00:11:26.849
and close a finding only when
a later look shows it fixed.

177
00:11:26.849 --> 00:11:28.240
And centrally?

178
00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.450
The same answer checks could run once,

179
00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:37.465
centrally, over every
agency's published file.

180
00:11:37.465 --> 00:11:40.478
That's the book's proposal,
not a federal program.

181
00:11:40.478 --> 00:11:44.602
It borrows one thing from Continuous
Diagnostics and Mitigation:

182
00:11:44.602 --> 00:11:47.827
a central view, with agencies
doing the remediation.

183
00:11:47.827 --> 00:11:52.401
But it reads public answers, not tool
output, so it can't see whether a

184
00:11:52.401 --> 00:11:57.889
credential exists, rotated or was revoked,
and it can't see AI nobody inventoried.

185
00:11:57.889 --> 00:12:02.877
A central scan is a good way to start a
conversation with every agency at once.

186
00:12:02.877 --> 00:12:04.440
It isn't a way to finish one.

187
00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:07.165
Governing the credential
stays with the agency,

188
00:12:07.165 --> 00:12:11.000
which brings us to what the
authorizing official needs to see.

189
00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:19.487
Every record so far ends up in one
place: the authorization package.

190
00:12:19.487 --> 00:12:22.962
First question: whose
boundary does the AI run in?

191
00:12:22.962 --> 00:12:28.362
Inside an authorized system, it's a new
component and a possible significant change.

192
00:12:28.362 --> 00:12:30.512
On its own, it's a new system.

193
00:12:30.512 --> 00:12:35.238
On a provider's certified cloud service,
the certification covers the provider,

194
00:12:35.238 --> 00:12:39.750
but the identities and grants that
service creates in your tenant are yours.

195
00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:44.311
Then the package answers five questions:
who owns it, how it signs in,

196
00:12:44.311 --> 00:12:48.125
what it can reach, who's watching,
and when its access ends.

197
00:12:48.125 --> 00:12:50.943
One gap to close: the control for services

198
00:12:50.943 --> 00:12:54.425
authenticating to each
other isn't in any baseline.

199
00:12:54.425 --> 00:12:59.780
So the system owner tailors it in, and the
authorizing official approves the plan.

200
00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:04.742
Then keep it current.

201
00:13:04.742 --> 00:13:10.067
AI governance adds three clocks beside the
annual security testing you already run.

202
00:13:10.067 --> 00:13:12.331
The inventory, at least once a year.

203
00:13:12.331 --> 00:13:16.780
The compliance plan, every two
years, with the next due in 2027,

204
00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:20.518
and it's easy to miss if you
only track the inventory.

205
00:13:20.518 --> 00:13:26.080
And waivers, certified every year and
reported within thirty days of any change.

206
00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:30.805
NIST's control overlays for AI aren't
out yet; there's no public draft.

207
00:13:30.805 --> 00:13:31.843
So don't wait.

208
00:13:31.843 --> 00:13:35.168
Tailor the baseline now and
compare when they're final.

209
00:13:35.168 --> 00:13:40.313
The decision: every waiver and pilot
certification becomes a governed exception

210
00:13:40.313 --> 00:13:45.080
with an expiry date, and one with no end
date goes to the authorizing official.

211
00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.290
Let's pull the decisions together.

212
00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:52.615
So, the decisions.

213
00:13:52.615 --> 00:13:56.359
System owners treat any agent
with no governed identity

214
00:13:56.359 --> 00:13:59.465
as a high-priority finding
the moment it's found.

215
00:13:59.465 --> 00:14:03.583
Authorizing officials authorize
AI through the existing process,

216
00:14:03.583 --> 00:14:07.627
before deployment, with both
high-impact signatures on one record.

217
00:14:07.627 --> 00:14:11.315
The Chief AI Officer links
every live inventory entry

218
00:14:11.315 --> 00:14:14.840
to an identity record and
runs the scan each vintage.

219
00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:17.278
The CIO budgets the licenses.

220
00:14:17.278 --> 00:14:21.245
System owners name an agency
employee as every sponsor,

221
00:14:21.245 --> 00:14:24.953
and raise each new tool or
model as a possible change.

222
00:14:24.953 --> 00:14:29.790
The CISO sets the rules: no production
secrets, no standing privilege.

223
00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:33.690
And no credential is issued before
its identity record exists.

224
00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:35.890
None of this needs a new authorization.

225
00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:40.153
AI Identity Governance has the
detail, every claim footnoted.

226
00:14:40.153 --> 00:14:40.980
Thank you.
